#!/bin/sh
# pre-merge-commit - the same checks, for the commits `git commit` never sees.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS (dark-factory-unification U5, review finding 5). The attestation
# checker skipped every merge commit (`rev-list --no-merges`) and said so in its header,
# calling merges "deliberately exempt" because `git merge` does not run pre-commit. That
# left a content channel nothing validated. What was actually true was narrower than the
# finding claimed, and was established by running it rather than by reading the docs:
#
#   CONFLICTED merge  -> git stops; the human resolves and runs `git commit`
#                     -> pre-commit RUNS, and attests. Already covered.
#   CLEAN merge       -> git commits by itself
#                     -> pre-merge-commit is the ONLY hook invoked. Nothing ran here.
#
# So conflict resolution was never the hole. The hole was the clean merge, whose tree is
# new content that no check had ever seen: two branches can each pass every check and
# still produce a merged tree that does not - a caller added on one side and a URL changed
# on the other is exactly the shape check-llm-gateway-routing.ps1 exists to catch, and
# neither commit alone contains it.
#
# It DELEGATES rather than duplicating. Two copies of a five-check list drift, and the
# copy that drifts is the one nobody looks at. `git write-tree` inside a clean merge yields
# the merge tree, so pre-commit's attestation step records exactly the right object with no
# special-casing.
#
# Activate in a fresh clone with:  git config core.hooksPath .githooks
exec "$(dirname "$0")/pre-commit"
